Impact of the summer 2025 heatwaves on emergency department visits in the USA
                        Amruta Nori-Sarma 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0003-2335-68111, Brianna M. Cartwright2, Antonella Zanobetti 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-8170-47363, Agnes Pastwa2, Nicholas Stucky 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-6610-53534 & …Mary D. Willis 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-0247-79835 Show authors

Nature Health
                             (2026) Cite this article









            Save article




                View saved research

AbstractDuring 22–25 June and 10–13 August 2025, heatwaves engulfed the eastern USA. Here we used continuously refreshed state-day counts of emergency department (ED) visits based on electronic health records, enabling near-real-time burden estimation during the same season to quantify impacts on ED utilization, to inform timely public health responses. Using electronic health records from 19 states (n = 8,169,637 ED visits), we evaluated associations between temperature and ED visits for summers 2023–2025 and conducted an estimation of summer 2025 health burdens. We used a conditional Poisson model with time strata (year × calendar month × day of week) and stratum-specific fixed effects. We estimated cumulative rate ratios and 95% confidence intervals (CIs), including attributable burdens. During the June heatwave, we estimated that 9.2% (95% CI 6.2–11.6%) of all-cause ED visits were attributable to heat (0–4-day lag). During the August heatwave, 8.3% (95% CI 6.0–10.1%) of all-cause ED visits were attributable to heat (0–4-day lag). Similar patterns were found for cumulative lags and for cause-specific outcomes. In this multistate study, extreme temperatures were associated with increased ED utilization, highlighting the need for near-real-time monitoring of heat-related health effects.

