aximum temperature (36.9°C) to the sample median (27°C), estimated separately by age group, US region, and sex. Points represent estimated rate ratios (centre); horizontal lines represent 95% confidence intervals derived from the multivariate normal distribution of DLNM coefficients. Separate conditional quasi-Poisson models were fit per subgroup with linear temperature basis, natural spline lag basis (df = 2), stratum fixed effects, and adjustment for public holidays. Sample sizes: 0-4 years (n = 530,211), 5-17 years (n = 870,348), 18-24 years (n = 874,574), 25-44 years (n = 2,550,345), 45-64 years (n = 1,873,249), 65+ years (n = 1,470,910); female (n = 4,638,457), male (n = 3,531,180); Midwest (n = 3,002,402), Northeast (n = 1,596,022), Southeast (n = 3,571,213).Source dataExtended Data Fig. 3 Descriptive exposure-response functions for the association between daily maximum temperature and cause-specific emergency department visit rates, April-August 2023-2025.Relative change in emergency department visit rates (log scale) as a function of daily maximum temperature, estimated using distributed lag non-linear models (DLNMs) with a natural spline exposure basis and natural spline lag basis pooled via multivariate random-effects meta-analysis across 19 states. Results are shown for (a) all-cause ED visits (n = 8,169,637), (b) kidney disease ED visits (n = 130,146), (c) mental health ED visits (n = 821,784), and (d) heat-related illness ED visits (n = 10,714). The solid line represents the estimated relative change in ED visit rate relative to the state-specific median daily maximum temperature; dashed lines represent 95% confidence intervals derived from the multivariate normal distribution of pooled DLNM coefficients. Rug marks at the bottom of each panel indicate the distribution of observed daily maximum temperature values. Models were fit using conditional quasi-Poisson regression with stratum fixed effects and adjustment for public holidays. Data are presented as estimated log-scale relative change (centre, solid line) with 95% confidence intervals (dashed lines).Source dataExtended Data Fig. 4 Sensitivity analysis using average daily temperature as the exposure metric for the association with all-cause emergency department visits, April-August 2023-2025.Results replicate the primary analysis substituting daily average temperature (Tavg) for daily maximum temperature (Tmax). (a) State-specific cumulative exposure-response functions (ERFs) for all-cause ED visits (n = 8,169,637 visits across 19 states) shown over each state’s observed temperature range; reference temperature = state-specific median daily average temperature (21.6°C). (b, left panel) Meta-analyzed cumulative ERF; dotted vertical line indicates reference temperature (21.6°C), dashed vertical line indicates 99th percentile (33.4°C). (b, right panel) Lag-specific rate ratios comparing the 99th percentile of daily average temperature (33.4°C) to the median (21.6°C). Shading and error bars represent 95% confidence intervals derived from the multivariate normal distribution of pooled DLNM coefficients. Data are presented as estimated cumulative rate ratios (centre) with 95% confidence intervals (shading/error bars). Models were fit using conditional quasi-Poisson regression with stratum fixed effects and adjustment for public holidays, cumulative effects summed over lags 0-4 days.Source dataExtended Data Fig. 5 Sensitivity analysis using a 7-day distributed lag window for the association between daily maximum temperature and all-cause emergency department visits, April-August 2023-2025.Results replicate the primary analysis extending the distributed lag from 4 days to 7 days. (a) State-specific cumulative exposure-response functions for all-cause ED visits (n = 8,169,637 visits across 19 states) shown over each state’s observed temperature range; reference temperature = state-specific median daily maximum temperature (27.1°C). (b, left panel) Meta-analyzed cumulative ERF; dotted vertical line indicates reference temperature (27.1°C), dashed vertical line indicates 99th percentile (40.4°C). (b, right panel) Lag-specific rate ratios comparing the 99th percentile of daily maximum temperature (40.4°C) to the median (27.1°C) across lags 0-7 days. Shading and error bars represent 95% confidence intervals derived from the multivariate normal distribution of pooled DLNM coefficients. Data are presented as estimated cumulative rate ratios (centre) with 95% confidence intervals (shading/error bars). Models were fit using conditional quasi-Poisson regression with stratum fixed effects and adjustment for public holidays.Source dataSupplementary informationSupplementary Information (download PDF )Supplementary Data Table 1.Reporting Summary (download PDF )Peer Review File (download PDF )Source dataSource Data Figs. 1–4, Source Data Extended Data Figs. 1–5 and Table 1 (download XLSX )Tab: Fig1_Maps. State-level summary statistics (average daily ED visits and average daily maximum temperature) underlying choropleth maps. Tab: Fig2_ERFs. Meta-analysed cumulative ERF estimates (temperature, rate ratio and 95% CI) by cause. Tab: Fig3_State_ERFs. State-specific cumulative ERF estimates for all-cause ED visits. Tab: Fig4_Subgroup_Forest. Subgroup-specific cumulative rate ratio estimates by age group, region and sex. Tab: ED_FigE1_LagResponse_byCause. Lag-specific rate ratio estimates by cause. Tab: ED_FigE2_Lag0_Subgroup. Lag 0 subgroup-specific rate ratio estimates by age group, region and sex. Tab: ED_FigE3_Descriptive_ERFs. Descriptive ERF estimates by cause. Tabs: ED_FigE4_Tavg_ERF, ED_FigE4_Tavg_LagResponse. State-specific and meta-analysed ERF estimates and lag–response estimates using average daily temperature. Tabs: ED_FigE5_7dayLag_ERF, ED_FigE5_7dayLag_LagResponse. State-specific and meta-analysed ERF estimates and lag–response estimates using a 7-day lag window. Tab: ED_TableE1_AttrFractions. Temperature-attributable fractions and at